Done. Fix is flashed, verified on the board, and only `main/main.c` changed.

## Root cause

`main/main.c`'s `send_with_retry()` allocated a fresh encoded buffer on every attempt:

```c
pkt.data = encode_frame(frame, len, &pkt.len);
...
esp_err_t err = link_send(&pkt);
if (err == ESP_OK) { return ESP_OK; }
/* <-- pkt.data from a failed attempt was never freed */
```

Per `link.h`, `link_send()` only takes ownership of `pkt->data` on `ESP_OK`; on any error the **caller still owns it**. The radio is busy on every 5th call (`link.c` returns `ESP_ERR_TIMEOUT`) and the queue can also reject, so roughly 20–25% of attempts leaked a 4096+8 = 4104-byte buffer. At 50 Hz that's ~50 KB/s of leaked heap.

Serial from the original firmware confirmed it: `heap_free` fell monotonically from 391916 → 14088 in ~7.5 s, then `sent` froze at 360 while `failed` climbed forever (`queue=0`, so the transmit task was healthy — capacity was fine, memory was not). Once the heap is too fragmented for a 4104-byte allocation, every frame fails permanently.

## Fix

Free the caller-owned buffer on the error path before retrying:

```c
esp_err_t err = link_send(&pkt);
if (err == ESP_OK) {
    return ESP_OK;
}
/* link_send only takes ownership of pkt.data on ESP_OK; on error the
 * caller still owns it, so free it before retrying. */
free(pkt.data);
ESP_LOGW(TAG, "send failed (%s), retrying", esp_err_to_name(err));
```

I did not touch `link.c`/`link.h`, the frame size, or the 50 Hz rate.

## Verification on the board

Reflashed the fixed build and ran 40 s: reached `stats seq=2000 sent=2001 retries=500 failed=0 queue=0 heap_free=391916` — heap exactly at its boot value, no decline, and sends never stop.
